{"id": "10530", "revid": "323822", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10530", "title": "East St. Louis", "text": "East St. Louis, commonly abbreviated as \"ESL\", is a city in Southern Illinois directly across the Mississippi River from St. Louis. It is a sprawling city with about 18,000 people, down from about 82,000 in the 1950s.\nWhile it attracts a few visitors who come mainly for gambling, East St. Louis is definitely not your typical travel destination. In 2013, it was considered to be the most dangerous city in the contiguous United States, based on violent crime statistics. A visit to ESL could be considered a way of getting a first-hand glimpse of the challenges that poverty-stricken U.S. urban areas face today.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nFour major interstates lead into East St. Louis. They are Interstate 70, Interstate 55, Interstate 255, and Interstate 64. Interstate 64 takes the most direct route into the city center. U.S. Route 40 connects East St. Louis to St. Louis, Missouri and runs through East St. Louis. U.S. Route 50 runs through ESL as well. Illinois Routes 3, 15, and 111 also enter and leave East St. Louis. The city is very accessible by road through many different routes.\nBy train.\nMetrolink.\nThe Metrolink train travels into East St. Louis on the blue and red lines. The Red Line begins at Lambert Airport in St. Louis, Missouri, and ends in Shiloh, Illinois. The Blue Line begins in Shrewsbury, MO and ends in Fairview Heights, Illinois. Both of these lines run through downtown St. Louis and both stop at the same East St. Louis stations- which are named East Riverfront, 5th and Missouri, Emerson Park, Jackie Joyner-Kersee Center, and Washington Park. For more information, fares, and schedules visit the Metrolink page.\nAmtrak.\nThe Amtrak Train does not directly station in East St. Louis, but makes stops nearby. The closest stop is in St. Louis, Missouri, at the 16th Street stop, approximately 5 miles (8 km) away. Other stops are the Kirkwood Amtrak Station (about 18 mi away) and Alton Amtrak Station (about 30 mi away). Taxi cabs are available at the station.\nBy plane.\nThere are many airports near East St. Louis. The largest nearby airport is the () approximately 16 mi from East St. Louis.\nThe Saint Louis Regional Airport () in St. Louis, Missouri, is around 25 mi from East St. Louis.\nA smaller airport is located in Mascoutah, Illinois, called Mid-America Airport[dead link] (), about 22 miles away.\nPublic-use airports closest to East St. Louis include: St. Louis Downtown Airport (), Sackman Field Airport, and St. Louis Metro East Airport.\nTaxis are available at all airports.\nGet around.\nThe Metro St. Louis website contains information about public transport in ESL as well as in St. Louis. ESL is a sprawling town, and the risk of sore feet is just one of the reasons why you shouldn't explore it on foot without a plan (see the Stay Safe section).\nEat &amp; drink.\nEast St. Louis has many of the typical fast-food restaurants, but for more upscale sit-down restaurants you're going to find a lot more if you cross the bridge to St. Louis.\nSleep.\nThe vast majority of travelers choose to sleep across the river in St. Louis, but there are some options in ESL and in Fairmont City.\nStay safe.\nWhile there are many friendly citizens anxious to preserve the community in East St. Louis, on the whole this is a very unsafe and dangerous area with high crime and drug rates. Visitors should avoid walking the streets at night. Make sure you are familiar with the area before arriving in East St. Louis, although there are areas that are secure. Do obtain clear and precise directions before driving around ESL, or use the security and reliability of mass transportation. The East St. Louis Park District and Illinois Southwest Tourism Bureau can assist with any questions."}
{"id": "10533", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=10533", "title": "East Stroudsburg", "text": "East Stroudsburg is a city in Monroe County, Pennsylvania.\nUnderstand.\nMonroe County was created on April 1, 1836, from parts of Northampton and Pike Counties and named for President James Monroe. At that time there was no county seat. Stroudsburg was finally selected to be the county seat on August 31, 1837. It was named after Jacob Stroud who was an original settler of the area.Today, Monroe County is comprised of 16-second class townships and 4 incorporated boroughs with a population of about 139,000 residents. Three of the boroughs and all of the townships were settled in the 18th century. The oldest of all the municipalities are Smithfield Township and the borough of East Stroudsburg.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe easiest way to get to East Stroudsburg by car is from Interstate 80. It is one of the last exits on the interstate before entering into New Jersey.\nBy bus.\nGreyhound provides one round-trip per day on a route between Scranton and Philadelphia. The Martz bus station, where the Greyhound buses make their stops, is located only a few miles away from Main Street.\nPO Box 1291 Stroudsburg, PA 18360\nMartz Trailways Rail Road 1\n+1 570-421-3040, +1-800-454-2487\nBy van.\nUptown Vans has several carriers operating to/from different stops in Downtown Stroudsburg and East Stroudsburg on routes traveling from Paterson and New York City. These stops are centrally located, though advance reservations are needed as the vans often sell out quickly.\nGet around.\nCar- The easiest way to get around town is by car. If you drive into town you can always use your own car, but there is always the option to rent a car as well once you get into town.\nEnterprise, 2493 Milford Rd Ste 100.\nBus- Although there is not a big use of buses in East Stroudsburg, there are buses that run through the town at different times making it easy to get around. The bus company that serves this town is Pocono Pony.\nSee.\nThis town is full of history and many other unique places and things to see. All over the town are different museums, art galleries, and nature resorts and parks.\nDo.\nEast Stroudsburg is located between two rivers, providing the town with many different things to do. Tourists can go camping, sky diving, golf, or take a walk in a park. Some of the best places to do these activities are:\nBuy.\nMain Street (Route 611) offers a lot of shopping opportunities. There are many different specialty, clothing, antique or gift shops all up and down the street. Just off Main Street, Crystal Street has just as many charming shops and boutiques to visit.\nEat.\nAll over the city are places to eat. There are restaurants that range from small take out and fast food places, to romantic restaurants. No matter what type of cuisine you are in the mood for, it can be found somewhere close by.\nDrink.\nEast Stroudsburg is not only home to many residents, but also considered to be a college town. East Stroudsburg University has about 8,000 students. Many of those students around campus and other parts of the town, so there are many bars and nightlife areas. Some of the most popular are:\nSleep.\nPlaces to stay in East Stroudsburg range from resorts to camping sights and everything in between. There are plenty of chain hotels with names that you would recognize, or smaller family owned and operated places for you to stay to get the most out of your visit. They are located all over the city, so whatever you are looking for, you will be able to find.\nSome of the most popular places to stay include:\nConnect.\nEast Stroudsburg Borough Municipal Building-\n24 Analomink Street, East Stroudsburg, PA 18301\nPhone: +1 570-421-8300\nPolice Department-\n+1 570-424-7002"}
